      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_b9318fa8",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_inequality_vs_drag_queens",
      "label": "The text 'we wouldn't have inequality' is mapped onto the drag queens as a representation of systemic inequality being dismissed as a result of individual effort.",
      "description": "The meme uses the visual of glamorous drag queens to represent the idea of 'working a bit' as a solution to systemic inequality. The text 'we wouldn't have inequality' is mapped onto the drag queens, implying that if they (or anyone like them) worked harder, inequality would disappear. This is an analogical mapping where the drag queens, as visually presented, are being used to represent the concept of individual effort being sufficient to solve systemic problems, thereby mocking the idea that systemic inequality can be resolved by personal effort alone. The drag queens' glamorous appearance contrasts with the cynical message, creating irony.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_8ecb0f5a"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_99ae5407",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the textual message as the implied subject of the economic critique",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the text 'maybe if you worked a bit we wouldn't have inequality' as the implied 'you' who is being addressed. The meme uses the text to directly address the viewer, suggesting that the viewer (the user) is the one who 'should have worked a bit' to solve systemic inequality. This projection is implicit, as the text uses 'you' as a deictic pronoun, directing the message at the audience. The user is thus projected onto the textual material, which frames them as the individual responsible for the systemic problem being discussed.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_8ecb0f5a"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_01efe8de",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme's message oversimplifies complex social and economic issues by framing systemic inequality as solvable through individual effort alone, which can be demoralizing and misleading. It implicitly dismisses the labor and resilience of marginalized communities, including those in the LGBTQ+ community who face systemic barriers, thus carrying implicit harmful undertones.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_8ecb0f5a"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_9a296168",
      "@type": "TargetCommunity",
      "instance_name": "person_1",
      "label": "Black drag queen",
      "description": "The meme visually centers on a Black drag queen, using her presence to anchor the critique of economic determinism. Her inclusion signals that the meme is targeting or commenting on the experiences of Black LGBTQ+ individuals, who face intersecting systemic barriers. The visual contrast between her glamorous drag persona and the cynical text about individual effort mocking systemic inequality implicitly frames her (and by extension, other marginalized drag performers) as emblematic of the very communities the meme's message dismisses.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_8ecb0f5a"
